---
// What is capacity planning? A plain answer page for people and answer
// engines: the definition first, then short questions, each answered in one
// paragraph that stands on its own. The FAQ structured data repeats the
// on-screen text word for word (built from the same array). No employer is
// named; everything links to the page that teaches it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import FromAnswer from '../components/FromAnswer.astro';
import { anyPost } from '../data/room.mjs';
import { load } from '../lib/paper.mjs';
import { DEFINITION, ENTITY, SCOPE_Q, SCOPE_A } from '../data/entity.mjs';
import { QUESTIONS } from '../data/questions.mjs';
// Where /questions/ already answers a question, reuse its words exactly, so the
// same question never gets two answers on this site (R14).
const Q = (id, link) => { const x = QUESTIONS.find((y) => y.id === id); if (!x) throw new Error(`what-is: unknown question ${id}`); return [x.q, `${x.a} ${link}`]; };
const { config } = load();
const site = config.site;

// [question, answer as HTML]. Keep each answer to one paragraph of short sentences.
const faq = [
  [SCOPE_Q, SCOPE_A],
  ['How is capacity planning different from capacity management?',
   'Capacity management, as ITIL describes it, is the wider practice of making sure services have the capacity and performance they need, now and later. Capacity planning is its forward-looking core. It decides what to add, where and when, often months or years ahead.'],
  Q('what-does-a-capacity-planner-do', 'See <a href="/guide/the-work-and-the-path/">The work and the path</a>.'),
  ['Why does capacity have to be planned so far ahead?',
   'Each layer of supply has its own lead time. Cloud capacity can arrive in minutes, machines in months, and buildings and power in years. A plan has to commit each layer before its lead time runs out, while demand is still uncertain. See <a href="/guide/concepts/supply-layers-and-lead-times/">supply layers and lead times</a> and <a href="/guide/concepts/commit-points/">commit points</a>.'],
  ['How is demand forecast for capacity?',
   'Organic growth can be forecast from a trend. Launches, migrations and new customers arrive as steps, so a trend misses them. The practice asks for a declaration instead: a signed statement of expected demand, which firms up in stages. See <a href="/guide/concepts/demand-and-declarations/">demand and declarations</a>.'],
  Q('plans-miss-when-forecast-was-right', 'See <a href="/guide/concepts/approval-and-allocation/">approval and allocation</a>.'),
  Q('what-is-a-coefficient', 'See <a href="/guide/concepts/the-coefficient/">the coefficient</a>.'),
  Q('plan-gpu-capacity', 'See <a href="/guide/settings/#ai-and-accelerator-fleets">AI and accelerator fleets</a>.'),
  ['Is there an open body of knowledge for infrastructure capacity planning?',
   `Yes. ${ENTITY} It has a <a href="/body-of-knowledge/">guide</a>, a <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>, <a href="/cases/">case studies</a> and a seed paper, and practitioners meet in <a href="/commons/">The Commons</a>.`],
];
const text = (h) => h.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
const url = new URL('/what-is-capacity-planning/', site.url).href;
const ld = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', '@id': url + '#faq', url, inLanguage: 'en',
    mainEntity: [['What is infrastructure capacity planning?', DEFINITION], ...faq].map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: text(a) } })) },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'DefinedTerm', name: 'Infrastructure capacity planning', alternateName: 'Capacity planning (infrastructure)', description: DEFINITION, url, inDefinedTermSet: new URL('/glossary/', site.url).href + '#set' },
];
---
<Base seoTitle="What is infrastructure capacity planning? A plain definition" title="What is capacity planning?" description="What infrastructure capacity planning is, in one sentence, with short answers on what planners do, lead times, demand forecasts and AI accelerator fleets." ld={ld} nav="bok">
  <div class="wrap wrap--reading">
    <div class="page-head reading">
      <p class="eyebrow">A plain answer</p>
      <h1>What is infrastructure capacity planning?</h1>
      <p class="lede"><strong>{DEFINITION}</strong></p>
      <p>Here, capacity planning means infrastructure, not people’s time on a team. It covers computing, storage, network and accelerators, and the space, power and contracts underneath them. It works across lead times from minutes to years, and across altitudes from a whole fleet down to one service.</p>
    </div>

    <FromAnswer post={anyPost()} topic="infrastructure capacity planning" />
    <section class="prose reading" aria-label="Questions and answers">
      {faq.map(([q, a], i) => (
        <>
          <h2 id={`q${i + 1}`}>{q}</h2>
          <p set:html={a} />
        </>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="prose reading" aria-labelledby="next-h">
      <h2 id="next-h">Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/questions/">More questions, answered</a>: forecasting launches, headroom, chargeback, FinOps and AI fleets.</li>
        <li><a href="/start/">Start by role</a>: a short reading route for your kind of work.</li>
        <li><a href="/guide/the-map/">The map</a>: the whole job on one page, by cycle and altitude.</li>
        <li><a href="/glossary/#your-words">Your words, our words</a>: the terms you already use, mapped to the guide.</li>
        <li><a href="/join/">Ask in The Commons</a>: one rule, don’t break your NDA.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <Cite title="What is infrastructure capacity planning?" url={url} date="18 November 2026" />
  </div>
</Base>
